    "knowledge_point": "Determining the Equation of a Wide Ellipse",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14095374/assets/84f42ca72c2a96a5ba03ca790bc3f0a6f6f5e582791aaf50e434bff16f77aed5.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?",
    "worked_solution": "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-4,0),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=5,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
